//   - Tinkers' Construct  -> Config.java: other mods' weapons work "without this mod having
//                            to know anything about Tinkers' Construct (or any other mod)". Declaring it
//                            would hint at special support that doesn't exist.
//   - EasyNPC             -> MonsterCommand.java: "there is no EasyNPC integration or dependency on
//                            anyone". It works because the tag is NBT and accepts any entity.
//   - AttributeFix        -> zero mentions in the whole repo.
//   - Configured, Customizable Player Models, Dynamic Lights [Server] -> table recommendations, without
//                            a single line of code in between. Dynamic Lights also FIXES
//                            a rule (see VisionManager), so it is documented in R4 — but
//                            recommending is not depending.
//
// All are OPTIONAL: with none, the mod behaves like vanilla wherever nothing is configured
// (invariant 9). The truly required ones are the addons', and those aren't published yet.
'''),
(303, 303, '''
//Real integrations: all four are in mods.toml with mandatory=false and have their own *Compat class.
'''),
(305, 306, '''
//Appearance packs: exactly the modids that the dndsheets/skins/ JSONs reference. This list
//is not about tastes — if a pack is added or removed, it changes with it.
'''),
(311, 314, '''
// The two that CANNOT be shared between platforms, and that is why they are separate:
//   - Cataclysm changes slug: 'lendercataclysm' on CurseForge, 'l_enders-cataclysm' on Modrinth.
//     Assuming it was the same cost a whole rejected upload (CurseForge error 1018).
//   - The Twilight Forest does NOT exist on Modrinth, it is CurseForge-exclusive.
'''),
(323, 324, '''
// The addons (dungeon, species) are separate jars and are uploaded separately: each one is a mod with its
// own modid, and species also requires Origins, which is not a dependency of the core.
'''),
(327, 336, '''
// Without a token, ModPublisher silently skips the platform and STILL prints "Published mod to all
// configured platforms" and exits successfully. Verified: with empty credentials there isn't even one HTTP
// request, yet the build says there was. That is worse than failing — someone believes they published and published
// nothing. This guard turns that silence into an error before the task does anything.
// To know whether Gradle is seeing the credentials without risking uploading anything:
//
//     ./gradlew checkPublishSecrets
//
// It prints ONLY whether they are present and how many characters long they are. Never the value: a token in a
// console's output ends up in a log, a screenshot or a history, and then it is no longer a secret.
'''),
(361, 362, '''
// Checks that the sample JSONs in test/dndsheets/ are still compatible with the mod's real
// parsers (see JsonContentSelfTest). It doesn't use the Forge runtime, so it runs outside the game.
'''),
]
SUBS = [
 ("//Cliente y servidor: este mod hace falta en los dos lados.", "//Client and server: this mod is needed on both sides."),
 ("'Dice si hay credenciales de publicacion, sin mostrarlas y sin publicar.'", "'Says whether publishing credentials are present, without showing them and without publishing.'"),
 ('"$platform: AUSENTE — pon su token en el entorno o en tu ~/.gradle/gradle.properties"', '"$platform: MISSING — put its token in the environment or in your ~/.gradle/gradle.properties"'),
 ('"No hay token de CurseForge: pon CURSE_TOKEN en el entorno o "', '"There is no CurseForge token: put CURSE_TOKEN in the environment or "'),
 ('"curseforgeToken en tu ~/.gradle/gradle.properties. Sin el, publishMod no sube nada y "', '"curseforgeToken in your ~/.gradle/gradle.properties. Without it, publishMod uploads nothing and "'),
 ('"aun asi termina con exito."', '"still finishes successfully."'),
]
apply('build.gradle', B, SUBS)

